      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The naturally occurring brain peptide Tyr-MIF-1 (Tyr-Pro-Leu-Gly-NH2) was tested for its ability to block and reverse the actions of morphine in the tail-flick test. Injected peripherally either 10 minutes before or after morphine, Tyr-MIF-1, like MIF-1, was found to significantly reduce the antinociceptive actions of morphine on thermal pain. The results indicate that Tyr-MIF-1 may act, in part, as an endogenous opiate antagonist."},
